r"""\subsection{Operational Settings and Scaling Regimes}\label{sec:param_scaling}
To characterize algorithmic stability and resource efficiency, we analyzed the empirical convergence behavior of GAC pipeline across three primary operational parameters:
\begin{enumerate}
 \item \textbf{Ensemble Size Setting ($M$):} In our main experiments, the base ensemble size is set to $M = 25$ as a practical default matching prior consensus literature. To systematically evaluate sensitivity, we tested a parameter surface spanning $M \in \{5, 10, 15, 25, 50\}$ across all six high-$D$ cohorts over 10 random seeds ($\{42, \dots, 51\}$, Exp~8f). At $M=5$, mean ARI is $0.168\text{--}0.198$; at $M=10$, it reaches $0.202\text{--}0.223$; and for $M \ge 15$, performance enters an asymptotic plateau ($0.227\text{--}0.228$ on these 10 seeds; $M=15$: $0.227$, $M=25$: $0.225$, $M=50$: $0.222$), confirming that modest ensemble sizes ($M \ge 15$) capture the full consensus benefit without requiring expensive larger ensembles. On the full 30 benchmark seeds ($\{0, \dots, 29\}$), the default configuration ($M=25, L=40$) yields the canonical benchmark mean of $0.217$.
 \item \textbf{Anchor Budget Considerations ($L$):} Bipartite anchor consensus constructs an affinity representation against $L$ landmark points. While larger $L$ provides denser anchor coverage, the generalized eigensolve on the anchor affinity matrix incurs cubic spectral complexity ($O(L^3)$). In our benchmarks, we fixed $L = 40$. Systematic sensitivity testing across $L \in \{20, 40, 60, 80\}$ (Exp~8f) across 10 random seeds ($\{42, \dots, 51\}$) reveals virtually flat response surfaces ($\Delta \le 0.003$ across $L \in [20, 80]$ for $M \ge 15$), where the baseline configuration ($M=25, L=40$) attains $0.225 \pm 0.128$; on the full 30 benchmark seeds ($\{0, \dots, 29\}$), $M=25, L=40$ settles at the reported omnibus benchmark mean of $0.217$, confirming that $L=40$ comfortably balances structural representation and computational efficiency.
 \item \textbf{Power-Law Scaling with Sample Size ($N$):} Across the controlled $N \in [10^4, 10^6]$ scaling experiments (Table~\ref{tab:exp5_controlled}), runtime scales with global empirical power-law exponent $\beta = 0.755 \pm 0.074$ (seed-bootstrap 95\% CI: $[0.648, 0.841]$) for Config A ($M=25, L=40$) and $\beta = 0.873 \pm 0.035$ (seed-bootstrap 95\% CI: $[0.825, 0.916]$) for Config B ($M=15, L=60$). Examining local exponents reveals approximately linear scaling up to $N=10^5$ ($\beta_{10^4 \to 10^5} \approx 0.43\text{--}0.55$), above which runtime growth steepens to $\beta_{10^5 \to 10^6} \approx 1.09\text{--}1.20$ as working memory approaches several gigabytes and cache effects become dominant. Bipartite graph compression avoids quadratic $O(N^2)$ storage and scales cleanly to one million points.
\end{enumerate}""",
